// The `prelude.math` sub-module: numeric operations beyond the arithmetic operators. The rounding
// trio is the only number -> integer conversion in the language (`/` always yields `number`).

@"The absolute value; preserves integer."
primitive agent abs[T extends number](value: T) -> T

@"The smaller of the two; preserves integer when both operands are integers."
primitive agent min[T extends number](left: T, right: T) -> T

@"The larger of the two; preserves integer when both operands are integers."
primitive agent max[T extends number](left: T, right: T) -> T

@"The largest integer not above the value. A non-finite value cannot occur (division never yields
one)."
primitive agent floor(value: number) -> integer

@"The smallest integer not below the value."
primitive agent ceil(value: number) -> integer

@"The nearest integer (half away from zero, the grade-school rule — not banker's rounding)."
primitive agent round(value: number) -> integer

@"@base@ raised to the @exponent@ power, as a `number`: a negative or fractional @exponent@ takes an
integer @base@ off the integers, so the type cannot promise integer — floor the result when an integer
is wanted. A non-finite result (0 to a negative power, a negative base to a fractional power) panics."
primitive agent pow(base: number, exponent: number) -> number
